{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794546169894,"sessionID":"ses_e2cb7c22dffeZNysJSTmWyN01Q","part":{"partID":"prt_1d34977ba0029qzYtTlU4MlxyH","sessionID":"ses_e2cb7c22dffeZNysJSTmWyN01Q","messageID":"msg_1d3496766001HIlGx4paIrAdy3","type":"tool","id":"call_00_vx8GETDTdUAhcUuMe4IA4399","tool":"read","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"path":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/commands/doctor/assets/doctor-setup-presentation.txt"},"output":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/commands/doctor/assets/doctor-setup-presentation.txt, lines 1-109\n1: # /doctor:setup Presentation Contract\n2: \n3: This file is the single source of truth for user-facing presentation in `/doctor:setup`: the argument failure, the doctor target list, the per-leg result table, result templates, and next-step text. The command router owns asset routing only. The workflow YAML owns execution behavior.\n4: \n5: ## 1. Argument Failure\n6: \n7: ```text\n8: Unknown argument: [argument]\n9: /doctor:setup takes no target and no flag. Run /doctor:setup on its own.\n10: STATUS=FAIL ERROR=\"unknown_argument\"\n11: ```\n12: \n13: ## 2. Doctor Target List\n14: \n15: Render this table first, before any leg runs.\n16: \n17: | Command | What it checks |\n18: |---------|----------------|\n19: | `/doctor:setup` | This overview: the doctor target list and the six read-only setup legs |\n20: | `/doctor:helper [<problem in plain words>] [--json]` | Rank the doctor commands that fit a described problem, with the class and the reason on every line |\n21: | `/doctor:env list` | The documented environment switches and where each value comes from |\n22: | `/doctor:update` | Whether a newer spec-kit release is available (bare runs the read-only check) |\n23: | `/doctor:git setup` | Whether the sk-git install, hooks, trust and standards are live |\n24: | `/doctor:git hooks` | Which shipped git hook gates are on or off, and switching one |\n25: | `/doctor:git standards` | The commit, PR and branch rules in .sk-git/ |\n26: | `/doctor:sk-code setup` | The sk-code setup battery, with a repair command per failing leg |\n27: | `/doctor:sk-code quality` | The repository quality config the gate reads with its tools, errors and disabled hygiene rules, then creating `.skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-quality/repository/quality.json` once |\n28: | `/doctor:ci report` | Lists every workflow with its switch state, trigger, filters and drift flags, read against the repository CI profile; `scaffold` and `toggle` are the confirmed action routes |\n29: | `/doctor:sk-skill check [<name-or-path>] [--json] [--into-hub <hub>]` | How far one skill, or the whole fleet, is from the Skilled format, row by row, with the repair for each finding |\n30: | `/doctor:sk-skill convert <name-or-path> [--dry-run]` | Moving one skill to the Skilled format: the plan, one yes per write, the backup, the write and the engine's undo block |\n31: | `/doctor:agents-md [check|embed|restore] [--json] [--root <dir>] [--dry-run]` | The root instruction file: which shipped sections are current, embedding the template over a printed plan and its digest, and putting one recorded run back |\n32: | `/doctor:speckit` | The generated trigger index, its lookup and the ripgrep recipes |\n33: | `/doctor:runtime-mirrors` | Whether each runtime's agent, command, prompt and hook mirrors match .skilled |\n34: | `/doctor:skill-advisor tune` | Advisor recommendation quality and scoring tuning |\n35: | `/doctor:skill-advisor rebuild` | Rebuilding the skill graph database |\n36: | `/doctor:skill-advisor skill-budget` | Skill description character budgets |\n37: | `/doctor:skill-advisor parent-skill` | A parent skill hub's structure and registry |\n38: | `/doctor:skill-advisor skill-graph-freshness` | Drift between the compiled, SQLite and on-disk skill graph |\n39: | `/doctor:skill-advisor router-reach` | Whether each declared routing phrase reaches its hub |\n40: | `/doctor:skill-advisor embedder` | Meaning matching for commands: whether the local embedder answers, what it costs, and the plan that pulls the model and starts the server after a yes |\n41: | `/doctor:deep-loop` | Deep-loop coverage graphs and convergence for research, review and council runs |\n42: | `/doctor:mcp` | Code Mode install and debug, its UTCP configuration and runtime registration |\n43: | `/doctor:install [--root <dir>] [--runtimes <list>] [--json]` | Putting Skilled into your own project repo: the build check, what is already linked, and the ordered setup commands to run |\n44: | `/doctor:symlink [check|link|unlink] [--json] [--root <dir>]` | The framework links in a project repo: which runtime folders are linked, broken or missing, linking them over a printed plan and its digest, and undoing one recorded run |\n45: | `/doctor:overlay [status|apply|repin] [--json] [--root <dir>]` | A vendored Skilled tree's own overlays: which destinations are out of sync and why, then putting them back, capturing one live edit, or re-pinning the tree to a newer upstream commit over a staged plan, after one yes |\n46: \n47: ## 3. Per-Leg Result Table\n48: \n49: Render one row per leg, in the workflow's order.\n50: \n51: | Leg | Command | Result | Repair |\n52: |-----|---------|--------|--------|\n53: | [leg] | [check-only command] | [PASS|FAIL|SKIP|DEGRADED|ERROR] | [the doctor command that repairs it or shows why, or none] |\n54: \n55: | Leg | Repair when it fails |\n56: |-----|----------------------|\n57: | sk-code | `/doctor:sk-code setup` |\n58: | sk-git | `/doctor:git setup` |\n59: | trigger-index | `/doctor:speckit` |\n60: | runtime-mirrors | `/doctor:runtime-mirrors` |\n61: | advisor | Stale build: `npm --prefix .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime run build`. Other FAIL or DEGRADED results: `/doctor:skill-advisor rebuild`. |\n62: | embedder | `/doctor:skill-advisor embedder` |\n63: \n64: ## 4. Result Templates\n65: \n66: ### Summary\n67: \n68: ```text\n69: DOCTOR DIAGNOSTIC RESULT\n70: Target: setup\n71: Status: [OK|FAIL|ERROR]\n72: Mutation class: read-only\n73: Evidence: [N] legs, [N] passed, [N] failed, [N] skipped, [N] degraded, [N] errors\n74: Recommendation: [the doctor command for each failing or degraded leg, or none]\n75: Last session hop: [the first line `session-relaunch.cjs history --last 1` printed, or no hop recorded yet]\n76: [open notes: N handoff notes not claimed yet, oldest <filename>; shown only when history printed it]\n77: [the line `session-relaunch.cjs window` printed: Claude context window: N tokens, or Claude context window: none configured with the two settings that supply one]\n78: [orphans: N, about M MB; list them with node .skilled/commands/doctor/scripts/orphan-process-check.cjs --reap]\n79: STATUS=[status]\n80: ```\n81: \n82: The last session hop line is informational. It shows when a session last handed over, kept, claimed or finished a handoff, and it never changes the status. The open notes line appears only while a handoff note is still open, so a hand-over nobody finished stays visible.\n83: The Claude context window line is informational and never changes the status. It shows the window the Claude relaunch nudge measures against. When no window is configured it names `autoCompactWindow` and `SESSION_RELAUNCH_WINDOW_TOKENS`, and the nudge stays off until one is set or a `[1m]` model runs.\n84: The orphan line is informational only and never changes the doctor status. A model server kept on purpose with `SPECKIT_HF_MODEL_SERVER_ORPHAN_REAP=0` also shows as unsupervised, so do not reap it. With no orphan processes, print `orphans: none`. Run the listed `--reap` command to see each process, then use its confirmation command with `--yes` only when those are the processes to stop.\n85: \n86: ### Failure Summary\n87: \n88: ```text\n89: DOCTOR TARGET FAILED\n90: Target: setup\n91: Step: [leg]\n92: Error: [message]\n93: Next: [recommended command or manual action]\n94: STATUS=ERROR ERROR=\"[message]\"\n95: ```\n96: \n97: ## 5. Troubleshooting Display\n98: \n99: | Situation | Suggested Action |\n100: |-----------|------------------|\n101: | A leg reports FAIL | Run the doctor command in that leg's Repair cell, then rerun `/doctor:setup` |\n102: | The advisor leg reports SKIP | Exit 75 has no `staleDistWarning` in stderr JSON. The advisor daemon is stopped because `--warm-only` does not start it |\n103: | The advisor leg reports FAIL for a stale build | Exit 75 includes `staleDistWarning` in stderr JSON. Run `npm --prefix .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime run build` |\n104: | The advisor leg reports DEGRADED | Exit 0 has `data.freshness` other than `live`. The skill graph is not live. Run `/doctor:skill-advisor rebuild` |\n105: | The sk-git leg reports DEGRADED | No sk-git check failed, but one is unknown or a gate is saved off. Run `/doctor:git setup` to see which. On a runtime the battery cannot name, the AI-tool check stays unknown |\n106: | The trigger-index leg reports stale documents | Run `/doctor:speckit` to see which documents are stale |\n107: | Route manifest parse error | Run `bash .skilled/commands/doctor/scripts/route-validate.sh` |\n108: \n109: End interactive presentations with: `What would you like to do next?`","title":"read","metadata":{"metadata":{"truncated":false},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/commands/doctor/assets/doctor-setup-presentation.txt, lines 1-109\n1: # /doctor:setup Presentation Contract\n2: \n3: This file is the single source of truth for user-facing presentation in `/doctor:setup`: the argument failure, the doctor target list, the per-leg result table, result templates, and next-step text. The command router owns asset routing only. The workflow YAML owns execution behavior.\n4: \n5: ## 1. Argument Failure\n6: \n7: ```text\n8: Unknown argument: [argument]\n9: /doctor:setup takes no target and no flag. Run /doctor:setup on its own.\n10: STATUS=FAIL ERROR=\"unknown_argument\"\n11: ```\n12: \n13: ## 2. Doctor Target List\n14: \n15: Render this table first, before any leg runs.\n16: \n17: | Command | What it checks |\n18: |---------|----------------|\n19: | `/doctor:setup` | This overview: the doctor target list and the six read-only setup legs |\n20: | `/doctor:helper [<problem in plain words>] [--json]` | Rank the doctor commands that fit a described problem, with the class and the reason on every line |\n21: | `/doctor:env list` | The documented environment switches and where each value comes from |\n22: | `/doctor:update` | Whether a newer spec-kit release is available (bare runs the read-only check) |\n23: | `/doctor:git setup` | Whether the sk-git install, hooks, trust and standards are live |\n24: | `/doctor:git hooks` | Which shipped git hook gates are on or off, and switching one |\n25: | `/doctor:git standards` | The commit, PR and branch rules in .sk-git/ |\n26: | `/doctor:sk-code setup` | The sk-code setup battery, with a repair command per failing leg |\n27: | `/doctor:sk-code quality` | The repository quality config the gate reads with its tools, errors and disabled hygiene rules, then creating `.skilled/skills/sk-code/sk-code-quality/repository/quality.json` once |\n28: | `/doctor:ci report` | Lists every workflow with its switch state, trigger, filters and drift flags, read against the repository CI profile; `scaffold` and `toggle` are the confirmed action routes |\n29: | `/doctor:sk-skill check [<name-or-path>] [--json] [--into-hub <hub>]` | How far one skill, or the whole fleet, is from the Skilled format, row by row, with the repair for each finding |\n30: | `/doctor:sk-skill convert <name-or-path> [--dry-run]` | Moving one skill to the Skilled format: the plan, one yes per write, the backup, the write and the engine's undo block |\n31: | `/doctor:agents-md [check|embed|restore] [--json] [--root <dir>] [--dry-run]` | The root instruction file: which shipped sections are current, embedding the template over a printed plan and its digest, and putting one recorded run back |\n32: | `/doctor:speckit` | The generated trigger index, its lookup and the ripgrep recipes |\n33: | `/doctor:runtime-mirrors` | Whether each runtime's agent, command, prompt and hook mirrors match .skilled |\n34: | `/doctor:skill-advisor tune` | Advisor recommendation quality and scoring tuning |\n35: | `/doctor:skill-advisor rebuild` | Rebuilding the skill graph database |\n36: | `/doctor:skill-advisor skill-budget` | Skill description character budgets |\n37: | `/doctor:skill-advisor parent-skill` | A parent skill hub's structure and registry |\n38: | `/doctor:skill-advisor skill-graph-freshness` | Drift between the compiled, SQLite and on-disk skill graph |\n39: | `/doctor:skill-advisor router-reach` | Whether each declared routing phrase reaches its hub |\n40: | `/doctor:skill-advisor embedder` | Meaning matching for commands: whether the local embedder answers, what it costs, and the plan that pulls the model and starts the server after a yes |\n41: | `/doctor:deep-loop` | Deep-loop coverage graphs and convergence for research, review and council runs |\n42: | `/doctor:mcp` | Code Mode install and debug, its UTCP configuration and runtime registration |\n43: | `/doctor:install [--root <dir>] [--runtimes <list>] [--json]` | Putting Skilled into your own project repo: the build check, what is already linked, and the ordered setup commands to run |\n44: | `/doctor:symlink [check|link|unlink] [--json] [--root <dir>]` | The framework links in a project repo: which runtime folders are linked, broken or missing, linking them over a printed plan and its digest, and undoing one recorded run |\n45: | `/doctor:overlay [status|apply|repin] [--json] [--root <dir>]` | A vendored Skilled tree's own overlays: which destinations are out of sync and why, then putting them back, capturing one live edit, or re-pinning the tree to a newer upstream commit over a staged plan, after one yes |\n46: \n47: ## 3. Per-Leg Result Table\n48: \n49: Render one row per leg, in the workflow's order.\n50: \n51: | Leg | Command | Result | Repair |\n52: |-----|---------|--------|--------|\n53: | [leg] | [check-only command] | [PASS|FAIL|SKIP|DEGRADED|ERROR] | [the doctor command that repairs it or shows why, or none] |\n54: \n55: | Leg | Repair when it fails |\n56: |-----|----------------------|\n57: | sk-code | `/doctor:sk-code setup` |\n58: | sk-git | `/doctor:git setup` |\n59: | trigger-index | `/doctor:speckit` |\n60: | runtime-mirrors | `/doctor:runtime-mirrors` |\n61: | advisor | Stale build: `npm --prefix .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime run build`. Other FAIL or DEGRADED results: `/doctor:skill-advisor rebuild`. |\n62: | embedder | `/doctor:skill-advisor embedder` |\n63: \n64: ## 4. Result Templates\n65: \n66: ### Summary\n67: \n68: ```text\n69: DOCTOR DIAGNOSTIC RESULT\n70: Target: setup\n71: Status: [OK|FAIL|ERROR]\n72: Mutation class: read-only\n73: Evidence: [N] legs, [N] passed, [N] failed, [N] skipped, [N] degraded, [N] errors\n74: Recommendation: [the doctor command for each failing or degraded leg, or none]\n75: Last session hop: [the first line `session-relaunch.cjs history --last 1` printed, or no hop recorded yet]\n76: [open notes: N handoff notes not claimed yet, oldest <filename>; shown only when history printed it]\n77: [the line `session-relaunch.cjs window` printed: Claude context window: N tokens, or Claude context window: none configured with the two settings that supply one]\n78: [orphans: N, about M MB; list them with node .skilled/commands/doctor/scripts/orphan-process-check.cjs --reap]\n79: STATUS=[status]\n80: ```\n81: \n82: The last session hop line is informational. It shows when a session last handed over, kept, claimed or finished a handoff, and it never changes the status. The open notes line appears only while a handoff note is still open, so a hand-over nobody finished stays visible.\n83: The Claude context window line is informational and never changes the status. It shows the window the Claude relaunch nudge measures against. When no window is configured it names `autoCompactWindow` and `SESSION_RELAUNCH_WINDOW_TOKENS`, and the nudge stays off until one is set or a `[1m]` model runs.\n84: The orphan line is informational only and never changes the doctor status. A model server kept on purpose with `SPECKIT_HF_MODEL_SERVER_ORPHAN_REAP=0` also shows as unsupervised, so do not reap it. With no orphan processes, print `orphans: none`. Run the listed `--reap` command to see each process, then use its confirmation command with `--yes` only when those are the processes to stop.\n85: \n86: ### Failure Summary\n87: \n88: ```text\n89: DOCTOR TARGET FAILED\n90: Target: setup\n91: Step: [leg]\n92: Error: [message]\n93: Next: [recommended command or manual action]\n94: STATUS=ERROR ERROR=\"[message]\"\n95: ```\n96: \n97: ## 5. Troubleshooting Display\n98: \n99: | Situation | Suggested Action |\n100: |-----------|------------------|\n101: | A leg reports FAIL | Run the doctor command in that leg's Repair cell, then rerun `/doctor:setup` |\n102: | The advisor leg reports SKIP | Exit 75 has no `staleDistWarning` in stderr JSON. The advisor daemon is stopped because `--warm-only` does not start it |\n103: | The advisor leg reports FAIL for a stale build | Exit 75 includes `staleDistWarning` in stderr JSON. Run `npm --prefix .skilled/skills/system-skill-advisor/runtime run build` |\n104: | The advisor leg reports DEGRADED | Exit 0 has `data.freshness` other than `live`. The skill graph is not live. Run `/doctor:skill-advisor rebuild` |\n105: | The sk-git leg reports DEGRADED | No sk-git check failed, but one is unknown or a gate is saved off. Run `/doctor:git setup` to see which. On a runtime the battery cannot name, the AI-tool check stays unknown |\n106: | The trigger-index leg reports stale documents | Run `/doctor:speckit` to see which documents are stale |\n107: | Route manifest parse error | Run `bash .skilled/commands/doctor/scripts/route-validate.sh` |\n108: \n109: End interactive presentations with: `What would you like to do next?`"}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"path\": \"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/.skilled/commands/doctor/assets/doctor-setup-presentation.txt\"}"},"time":{"start":1794546169786,"end":1794546169894}}}}
{"type":"step_finish","timestamp":1794546171273,"sessionID":"ses_e2cb7c22dffeZNysJSTmWyN01Q","part":{"id":"prt_1d3497d89001C1dnpPjLZ9V0Y4","sessionID":"ses_e2cb7c22dffeZNysJSTmWyN01Q","messageID":"msg_1d3496766001HIlGx4paIrAdy3","type":"step-finish","reason":"tool-calls","snapshot":"534662561c27836ac2da849b6d304478113e2c09","cost":0,"tokens":{"input":21147,"output":72,"reasoning":573,"cache":{"read":12416,"write":0}}}}
{"type":"step_start","timestamp":1794546173426,"sessionID":"ses_e2cb7c22dffeZNysJSTmWyN01Q","part":{"id":"prt_1d34985f2001HuqTCQIGY4r26Z","sessionID":"ses_e2cb7c22dffeZNysJSTmWyN01Q","messageID":"msg_1d3497d8f001FSN0brXVVeLYWW","type":"step-start","snapshot":"df7ffc75080115561438582df8a1582f76aa893e"}}
{"type":"tool_use","timestamp":1794546179436,"sessionID":"ses_e2cb7c22dffeZNysJSTmWyN01Q","part":{"partID":"prt_1d3499d42001NwKS82cyHO10nI","sessionID":"ses_e2cb7c22dffeZNysJSTmWyN01Q","messageID":"msg_1d3497d8f001FSN0brXVVeLYWW","type":"tool","id":"call_01_wRZ51PFAEOHuWf9i8kK35160","tool":"read","state":{"status":"completed","input":{"path":"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/REPO RULES.md"},"output":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/REPO RULES.md, lines 1-108\n1: # REPO RULES\n2: \n3: Repo-local operating rules. `AGENTS.md` **Gate 5 (§2)** makes reading this mandatory\n4: before your first write of the session; this document then routes you to every rule\n5: file that governs what you are about to do. **It is a router, not a rulebook:** it\n6: holds no rules of its own.\n7: \n8: ---\n9: \n10: ## 1. HOW TO USE THIS\n11: \n12: 1. **Match on the action you are about to take**, not the topic of the request.\n13: 2. **Load before the action.** A rule read afterwards is a post-mortem.\n14: 3. **A file already in context is not re-read.**\n15: 4. **Every trigger that fires is loaded, not just the first.** They compose; the more\n16:    specific wins on conflict. Three and four firing at once is the normal case, not an\n17:    edge case.\n18: 5. **Nothing fires → `AGENTS.md` alone governs.** Do not hunt for a rule to apply.\n19: \n20: ### Precedence\n21: \n22: | Level | Source | Can be overridden? |\n23: |-------|--------|--------------------|\n24: | 1 | Every `AGENTS.md` §1 hard blocker, the Four Laws, PLAN-WORKFLOW LOCK, Comment Hygiene, and every mandatory gate in §2 | No |\n25: | 2 | An explicit, in-the-moment operator instruction | Not applicable, it is the instruction |\n26: | 3 | These rule files, and the guidance in a skill's `SKILL.md` | Only by level 1 or 2 |\n27: | 4 | General judgment | By anything above |\n28: \n29: A rule file may tighten `AGENTS.md`. None relaxes a HARD BLOCK or authorizes what\n30: `AGENTS.md` forbids. Gate 5 does not change that: it makes the **load** mandatory,\n31: while what you load stays at level 3, the obligation to read is tier 1, the content\n32: is not.\n33: \n34: ---\n35: \n36: ## 2. TRIGGER TABLE\n37: \n38: | You are about to… | Load | It settles |\n39: |-------------------|------|-----------|\n40: | Add a file, module, class, interface, option, layer, or dependency · write a spec or plan that adds one · generalize something that works · write \"flexible\", \"future-proof\", \"scalable\", \"extensible\", \"might need\", \"best practice\", \"while we're here\" · add a test beyond the coverage floor | [`prevent-overengineering.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/prevent-overengineering.md) | Whether this should exist at all, and at what size |\n41: | Touch a file outside the ask · fix something noticed in passing · change something else because it would make the fix easier · rename, reformat, or delete beyond the named area · deviate from an approved plan · decide what to do with the rest when part of the work is blocked · notice a defect, smell or stale comment outside the files in scope | [`scope-discipline.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/scope-discipline.md) | What is yours to change, and how to raise what isn't |\n42: | Say \"done\", \"works\", \"fixed\", \"passing\", \"no regressions\" · report a result · quote a number · act on a tool's or sub-agent's success report · close out a turn · report a file path or command output · write a completion summary or tick something off as done | [`evidence-and-proof.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/evidence-and-proof.md) | What counts as proof, how a green run lies, what an honest close-out contains |\n43: | Decide whether to hand work to another runtime at all · hand it to a CLI executor, sub-agent, fan-out lineage, or deep loop · compose the prompt one will act on · accept or quote what one returned · answer a judgment question from your own reading alone | [`delegation-and-orchestration.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/delegation-and-orchestration.md) | The orchestrating posture, what a brief must carry, why one model is one opinion |\n44: | Delete, overwrite, migrate, deploy, publish, send, install · force-push or rewrite history · change a shared contract · touch auth, data, or config · make any call that leaves this machine · truncate · touch branches, tags, or reflogs | [`blast-radius.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/blast-radius.md) | Reversibility, the rollback sentence, when to stop for a yes |\n45: | Diagnose a failure · make a red check green · attempt the same fix twice · add a special case, retry, sleep, or broadened catch · call a failure a flake, infra, or pre-existing | [`root-cause-and-debugging.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/root-cause-and-debugging.md) | Fixing the producer instead of the symptom, and when to level up to the seam |\n46: | Answer without certainty · contradict the operator · fill a gap with a plausible guess · hit a contradiction between two things that must both be true · name a path, flag, function, version, or number you have not verified · not know while a plausible answer is available · see sources disagree, or the code contradict the spec, the docs or the operator · the operator asserts something you believe is wrong · change a checked answer after pushback or doubt, or drop an approach before it concludes · cite a rule, restriction, policy, limit or permission, or decline a request | [`uncertainty-and-honesty.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/uncertainty-and-honesty.md) | Confidence bands, UNKNOWN, contradiction halts |\n47: | Write any substantive reply, an answer, an explanation, a close-out or a status · draft or edit any sentence a reader will read · present a recommendation, a fork, or a trade-off · start a multi-step stretch of work the reader will not see inside · report what a long autonomous run found · the reader says they did not follow or asks for a plainer version | [`communication.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/communication.md) | How a reply reads: answer first, one recommended path, length, filler, plain sentences, punctuation, and no table in a reply |\n48: | End a turn · report work done, blocked, or partly done · end a turn while something you started is still running · ask the operator anything · end a turn on a fork or two acceptable paths · continue past a decision nobody made | [`communication-handoff.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/communication-handoff.md) | What is the operator's to do next, what is still running and what resumes it, and when a question becomes a structured choice |\n49: | Wire, rewire or remove a mode in a parent-hub skill · edit a hub's registry, router, root `ROUTER.md`, `graph-metadata.json` or `SKILL.md` mode table, or a mode packet's own `ROUTER.md` · report that a mode is registered, routed, reachable or integrated · quote a per-hub gate result · create a new skill under a hub, or refactor one that already lives there | [`skill-hub-routing.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/skill-hub-routing.md) | The two routing stages, what \"integrated\" requires, and checking the hub you actually changed |\n50: \n51: ---\n52: \n53: ## 3. INDEX\n54: \n55: | Rule | Summary |\n56: |------|---------|\n57: | [Overengineering](.skilled/repo-rules/prevent-overengineering.md) | Build the smallest thing that solves the stated problem; take a costlier move only by naming what fails at the cheaper one. |\n58: | [Scope discipline](.skilled/repo-rules/scope-discipline.md) | The requested scope is the deliverable. Adjacent problems get named, not fixed. |\n59: | [Evidence and proof](.skilled/repo-rules/evidence-and-proof.md) | A claim is only as strong as the observation behind it, and a receipt confirms only what could have contradicted it. Distinguish observed from derived from inferred. |\n60: | [Delegation and orchestration](.skilled/repo-rules/delegation-and-orchestration.md) | Delegating makes you the orchestrator; brief with evidence, and no single model's verdict closes a question. |\n61: | [Blast radius](.skilled/repo-rules/blast-radius.md) | Size effort to what the change can break; no irreversible step without a named rollback and a yes. |\n62: | [Root cause](.skilled/repo-rules/root-cause-and-debugging.md) | Fix the producer, not the symptom; every fix names the mechanism that caused the failure. |\n63: | [Uncertainty and honesty](.skilled/repo-rules/uncertainty-and-honesty.md) | Never fabricate; mark the confidence you actually have and halt on a contradiction. |\n64: | [Communication](.skilled/repo-rules/communication.md) | Write so the reader can act after one pass: no table in a reply, nothing that does not carry information. |\n65: | [Communication handoff](.skilled/repo-rules/communication-handoff.md) | End every turn by naming what is now the operator's to do, in the form that lets them do it. |\n66: | [Hub routing](.skilled/repo-rules/skill-hub-routing.md) | A hub routes in two stages, so a mode registered on one surface is not reachable, and a gate run without its hub argument reports on a hub you did not touch. |\n67: \n68: Each file expands `AGENTS.md` and is bounded by it: where a rule file appears to\n69: permit something `AGENTS.md` restricts, `AGENTS.md` wins and the rule file is wrong.\n70: \n71: ---\n72: \n73: ## 4. SCOPE OF THIS DOCUMENT\n74: \n75: **In:** how to think and act, restraint, scope, evidence, risk, diagnosis, honesty,\n76: the posture to hold when work is handed to another runtime, how the resulting reply\n77: reads, what you may claim about wiring you have changed, and how a turn hands control\n78: back to the operator. The delegation posture was the first widening and Delivery the\n79: second, added when `AGENTS.md` §8 moved down. Delivery is one of two rules here whose\n80: trigger is a whole turn rather than a specific action, the other being the handback rule,\n81: which fires at every turn's end, and `AGENTS.md` §4, under Reply Rules and Mandates, keeps\n82: the two clauses that must bind even when nothing loads.\n83: \n84: **Out:** skill routing, workflow selection, spec-folder mechanics, and the *mechanics*\n85: of agent and CLI dispatch: which agent, which command, which model, which flags.\n86: Those belong to `AGENTS.md` §2 and the skills it routes to, and are deliberately\n87: absent here so each has exactly one place to change. The line is between plumbing and\n88: posture: how to dispatch is theirs, how to think while dispatching is ours.\n89: \n90: **The routing carve-out, added deliberately as the third widening.** *Selecting* a\n91: route stays Out. *Verifying wiring you changed, and what you may claim about it*, is\n92: In; it is an evidence obligation whose subject happens to be routing. `skill-hub-routing.md`\n93: is the one rule on this side of that line, and it carries no route-selection guidance:\n94: the mechanics stay in the skills, and the rule points at them. A fourth widening that\n95: admits selection itself would dissolve the boundary; this one does not.\n96: \n97: **The ask-surface carve-out, added as the fourth widening, on an operator decision that\n98: overrode a research refusal.** A four-iteration decision-test run returned *refuse* here:\n99: naming a question tool per runtime reads as the \"which command\" family, and the paragraph\n100: above pre-refuses a fourth widening that admits selection. The operator widened it anyway,\n101: and what follows is the narrowing that keeps the boundary standing. *Choosing between\n102: runtimes, agents, commands, models or flags* stays Out, unchanged. *Naming the surface that\n103: asks the operator a question, in the runtime you are already running in*, is In. That is not\n104: a selection, because nothing is being selected: the runtime is a given, and the rule records\n105: which of its surfaces carries an obligation the rule already imposes. `communication-handoff.md`\n106: is the one rule on this side of that line, its §5 is the only place a tool name appears, and\n107: it names only what this repository confirms. A fifth widening that let a rule pick between\n108: runtimes would be the dissolution this one avoids.","title":"read","metadata":{"metadata":{"truncated":false},"content":[{"type":"text","text":"Read file /Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/REPO RULES.md, lines 1-108\n1: # REPO RULES\n2: \n3: Repo-local operating rules. `AGENTS.md` **Gate 5 (§2)** makes reading this mandatory\n4: before your first write of the session; this document then routes you to every rule\n5: file that governs what you are about to do. **It is a router, not a rulebook:** it\n6: holds no rules of its own.\n7: \n8: ---\n9: \n10: ## 1. HOW TO USE THIS\n11: \n12: 1. **Match on the action you are about to take**, not the topic of the request.\n13: 2. **Load before the action.** A rule read afterwards is a post-mortem.\n14: 3. **A file already in context is not re-read.**\n15: 4. **Every trigger that fires is loaded, not just the first.** They compose; the more\n16:    specific wins on conflict. Three and four firing at once is the normal case, not an\n17:    edge case.\n18: 5. **Nothing fires → `AGENTS.md` alone governs.** Do not hunt for a rule to apply.\n19: \n20: ### Precedence\n21: \n22: | Level | Source | Can be overridden? |\n23: |-------|--------|--------------------|\n24: | 1 | Every `AGENTS.md` §1 hard blocker, the Four Laws, PLAN-WORKFLOW LOCK, Comment Hygiene, and every mandatory gate in §2 | No |\n25: | 2 | An explicit, in-the-moment operator instruction | Not applicable, it is the instruction |\n26: | 3 | These rule files, and the guidance in a skill's `SKILL.md` | Only by level 1 or 2 |\n27: | 4 | General judgment | By anything above |\n28: \n29: A rule file may tighten `AGENTS.md`. None relaxes a HARD BLOCK or authorizes what\n30: `AGENTS.md` forbids. Gate 5 does not change that: it makes the **load** mandatory,\n31: while what you load stays at level 3, the obligation to read is tier 1, the content\n32: is not.\n33: \n34: ---\n35: \n36: ## 2. TRIGGER TABLE\n37: \n38: | You are about to… | Load | It settles |\n39: |-------------------|------|-----------|\n40: | Add a file, module, class, interface, option, layer, or dependency · write a spec or plan that adds one · generalize something that works · write \"flexible\", \"future-proof\", \"scalable\", \"extensible\", \"might need\", \"best practice\", \"while we're here\" · add a test beyond the coverage floor | [`prevent-overengineering.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/prevent-overengineering.md) | Whether this should exist at all, and at what size |\n41: | Touch a file outside the ask · fix something noticed in passing · change something else because it would make the fix easier · rename, reformat, or delete beyond the named area · deviate from an approved plan · decide what to do with the rest when part of the work is blocked · notice a defect, smell or stale comment outside the files in scope | [`scope-discipline.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/scope-discipline.md) | What is yours to change, and how to raise what isn't |\n42: | Say \"done\", \"works\", \"fixed\", \"passing\", \"no regressions\" · report a result · quote a number · act on a tool's or sub-agent's success report · close out a turn · report a file path or command output · write a completion summary or tick something off as done | [`evidence-and-proof.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/evidence-and-proof.md) | What counts as proof, how a green run lies, what an honest close-out contains |\n43: | Decide whether to hand work to another runtime at all · hand it to a CLI executor, sub-agent, fan-out lineage, or deep loop · compose the prompt one will act on · accept or quote what one returned · answer a judgment question from your own reading alone | [`delegation-and-orchestration.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/delegation-and-orchestration.md) | The orchestrating posture, what a brief must carry, why one model is one opinion |\n44: | Delete, overwrite, migrate, deploy, publish, send, install · force-push or rewrite history · change a shared contract · touch auth, data, or config · make any call that leaves this machine · truncate · touch branches, tags, or reflogs | [`blast-radius.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/blast-radius.md) | Reversibility, the rollback sentence, when to stop for a yes |\n45: | Diagnose a failure · make a red check green · attempt the same fix twice · add a special case, retry, sleep, or broadened catch · call a failure a flake, infra, or pre-existing | [`root-cause-and-debugging.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/root-cause-and-debugging.md) | Fixing the producer instead of the symptom, and when to level up to the seam |\n46: | Answer without certainty · contradict the operator · fill a gap with a plausible guess · hit a contradiction between two things that must both be true · name a path, flag, function, version, or number you have not verified · not know while a plausible answer is available · see sources disagree, or the code contradict the spec, the docs or the operator · the operator asserts something you believe is wrong · change a checked answer after pushback or doubt, or drop an approach before it concludes · cite a rule, restriction, policy, limit or permission, or decline a request | [`uncertainty-and-honesty.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/uncertainty-and-honesty.md) | Confidence bands, UNKNOWN, contradiction halts |\n47: | Write any substantive reply, an answer, an explanation, a close-out or a status · draft or edit any sentence a reader will read · present a recommendation, a fork, or a trade-off · start a multi-step stretch of work the reader will not see inside · report what a long autonomous run found · the reader says they did not follow or asks for a plainer version | [`communication.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/communication.md) | How a reply reads: answer first, one recommended path, length, filler, plain sentences, punctuation, and no table in a reply |\n48: | End a turn · report work done, blocked, or partly done · end a turn while something you started is still running · ask the operator anything · end a turn on a fork or two acceptable paths · continue past a decision nobody made | [`communication-handoff.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/communication-handoff.md) | What is the operator's to do next, what is still running and what resumes it, and when a question becomes a structured choice |\n49: | Wire, rewire or remove a mode in a parent-hub skill · edit a hub's registry, router, root `ROUTER.md`, `graph-metadata.json` or `SKILL.md` mode table, or a mode packet's own `ROUTER.md` · report that a mode is registered, routed, reachable or integrated · quote a per-hub gate result · create a new skill under a hub, or refactor one that already lives there | [`skill-hub-routing.md`](.skilled/repo-rules/skill-hub-routing.md) | The two routing stages, what \"integrated\" requires, and checking the hub you actually changed |\n50: \n51: ---\n52: \n53: ## 3. INDEX\n54: \n55: | Rule | Summary |\n56: |------|---------|\n57: | [Overengineering](.skilled/repo-rules/prevent-overengineering.md) | Build the smallest thing that solves the stated problem; take a costlier move only by naming what fails at the cheaper one. |\n58: | [Scope discipline](.skilled/repo-rules/scope-discipline.md) | The requested scope is the deliverable. Adjacent problems get named, not fixed. |\n59: | [Evidence and proof](.skilled/repo-rules/evidence-and-proof.md) | A claim is only as strong as the observation behind it, and a receipt confirms only what could have contradicted it. Distinguish observed from derived from inferred. |\n60: | [Delegation and orchestration](.skilled/repo-rules/delegation-and-orchestration.md) | Delegating makes you the orchestrator; brief with evidence, and no single model's verdict closes a question. |\n61: | [Blast radius](.skilled/repo-rules/blast-radius.md) | Size effort to what the change can break; no irreversible step without a named rollback and a yes. |\n62: | [Root cause](.skilled/repo-rules/root-cause-and-debugging.md) | Fix the producer, not the symptom; every fix names the mechanism that caused the failure. |\n63: | [Uncertainty and honesty](.skilled/repo-rules/uncertainty-and-honesty.md) | Never fabricate; mark the confidence you actually have and halt on a contradiction. |\n64: | [Communication](.skilled/repo-rules/communication.md) | Write so the reader can act after one pass: no table in a reply, nothing that does not carry information. |\n65: | [Communication handoff](.skilled/repo-rules/communication-handoff.md) | End every turn by naming what is now the operator's to do, in the form that lets them do it. |\n66: | [Hub routing](.skilled/repo-rules/skill-hub-routing.md) | A hub routes in two stages, so a mode registered on one surface is not reachable, and a gate run without its hub argument reports on a hub you did not touch. |\n67: \n68: Each file expands `AGENTS.md` and is bounded by it: where a rule file appears to\n69: permit something `AGENTS.md` restricts, `AGENTS.md` wins and the rule file is wrong.\n70: \n71: ---\n72: \n73: ## 4. SCOPE OF THIS DOCUMENT\n74: \n75: **In:** how to think and act, restraint, scope, evidence, risk, diagnosis, honesty,\n76: the posture to hold when work is handed to another runtime, how the resulting reply\n77: reads, what you may claim about wiring you have changed, and how a turn hands control\n78: back to the operator. The delegation posture was the first widening and Delivery the\n79: second, added when `AGENTS.md` §8 moved down. Delivery is one of two rules here whose\n80: trigger is a whole turn rather than a specific action, the other being the handback rule,\n81: which fires at every turn's end, and `AGENTS.md` §4, under Reply Rules and Mandates, keeps\n82: the two clauses that must bind even when nothing loads.\n83: \n84: **Out:** skill routing, workflow selection, spec-folder mechanics, and the *mechanics*\n85: of agent and CLI dispatch: which agent, which command, which model, which flags.\n86: Those belong to `AGENTS.md` §2 and the skills it routes to, and are deliberately\n87: absent here so each has exactly one place to change. The line is between plumbing and\n88: posture: how to dispatch is theirs, how to think while dispatching is ours.\n89: \n90: **The routing carve-out, added deliberately as the third widening.** *Selecting* a\n91: route stays Out. *Verifying wiring you changed, and what you may claim about it*, is\n92: In; it is an evidence obligation whose subject happens to be routing. `skill-hub-routing.md`\n93: is the one rule on this side of that line, and it carries no route-selection guidance:\n94: the mechanics stay in the skills, and the rule points at them. A fourth widening that\n95: admits selection itself would dissolve the boundary; this one does not.\n96: \n97: **The ask-surface carve-out, added as the fourth widening, on an operator decision that\n98: overrode a research refusal.** A four-iteration decision-test run returned *refuse* here:\n99: naming a question tool per runtime reads as the \"which command\" family, and the paragraph\n100: above pre-refuses a fourth widening that admits selection. The operator widened it anyway,\n101: and what follows is the narrowing that keeps the boundary standing. *Choosing between\n102: runtimes, agents, commands, models or flags* stays Out, unchanged. *Naming the surface that\n103: asks the operator a question, in the runtime you are already running in*, is In. That is not\n104: a selection, because nothing is being selected: the runtime is a given, and the rule records\n105: which of its surfaces carries an obligation the rule already imposes. `communication-handoff.md`\n106: is the one rule on this side of that line, its §5 is the only place a tool name appears, and\n107: it names only what this repository confirms. A fifth widening that let a rule pick between\n108: runtimes would be the dissolution this one avoids."}],"providerCall":{"executed":false},"providerResult":{"executed":false},"rawInput":"{\"path\": \"/Users/michelkerkmeester/worktrees/public/352-command-surfacing-research/REPO RULES.md\"}"},"time":{"start":1794546179394,"end":1794546179436}}}}
